<!--
  ClickHouse sized for its cgroup rather than for the host.

  ClickHouse reads /proc/meminfo to decide how large its caches should be. Inside a
  container that file describes the host, so a server under a 1 GiB cgroup limit sizes
  itself for 11.7 GiB, allocates accordingly, and is OOM-killed by the kernel the first
  time a real query lands. The symptom is a restart loop with nothing in the application
  log, because the process is killed rather than failing.

  These four values are the ones that matter at this size. They are a mounted file rather
  than a shell string in the compose command because a config built by `echo` inside a
  YAML scalar is not reviewable, and this one is load-bearing.

  Task ids: M32.1.1.2
-->
<clickhouse>
    <!-- 768 MiB against a 1024 MiB cgroup limit. The 256 MiB difference is not slack: it
         is the page cache, the merge buffers and the allocator's fragmentation, none of
         which are counted by max_server_memory_usage but all of which are counted by the
         cgroup. Setting this to the cgroup limit is the common mistake and it produces
         exactly the kill this file exists to prevent. Bytes, because the server takes
         bytes here and silently ignores a suffix. -->
    <max_server_memory_usage>805306368</max_server_memory_usage>

    <!-- The mark cache is the one cache worth keeping at this size; it is what stops a
         repeated query re-reading index granules from disk. 128 MiB is small, and it is
         a sixth of the server allowance rather than the default fraction of a machine
         this server does not have. -->
    <mark_cache_size>134217728</mark_cache_size>

    <!-- Off. The uncompressed cache pays for itself on repeated point lookups over hot
         data, and a trace store is written far more than it is read. At this size the
         memory is better spent on merges keeping up with ingest. -->
    <uncompressed_cache_size>0</uncompressed_cache_size>

    <!-- Merges are the background work that decides whether ingest keeps up. The default
         scales with core count and would start more concurrent merges than 768 MiB can
         hold buffers for; four is what fits. Falling behind here is not an error, it is
         query latency climbing over days, which is why it is set rather than left. -->
    <background_pool_size>4</background_pool_size>
</clickhouse>
